 "texts": [
  {
   "profile": "J1",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## What photosynthesis is\n- No rush: photosynthesis is the process plants use to make their own food.\n- A plant takes in carbon dioxide from the air through tiny openings in its leaves.\n- It takes in water from the soil through its roots.\n- Using light energy from the sun, it turns carbon dioxide and water into glucose.\n- Glucose is the plant's food, used to grow and to store energy.\n\n## Where it happens and why leaves are green\n- Photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves.\n- Inside leaf cells are small green structures called chloroplasts.\n- Chloroplasts contain a green pigment called chlorophyll.\n- Chlorophyll absorbs mostly red and blue light and reflects green light.\n- That reflection is why leaves look green.\n- Without chlorophyll the plant could not capture the light energy it needs.\n\n## Oxygen by-product and word equation\n- Oxygen is released as a by-product of photosynthesis.\n- The oxygen leaves the plant through the same tiny openings in the leaves.\n- Animals and people breathe in this oxygen.\n- Word equation: carbon dioxide plus water, using light energy, makes glucose plus oxygen.\n\n## What limits the process\n- Too little light, water or carbon dioxide means less glucose and slower growth.\n- A plant kept in a dark cupboard turns pale and weak.",
    "mirror": "## What Photosynthesis Is\n- Photosynthesis is the process plants use to make their own food.\n- A plant takes in carbon dioxide from the air through tiny openings in its leaves.\n- A plant takes in water from the soil through its roots.\n- Using light energy from the sun, the plant turns carbon dioxide and water into glucose, a kind of sugar.\n- The glucose is the plant's food, and the plant uses it to grow and to store energy.\n- Using light energy from the sun, the plant turns carbon dioxide and water into glucose, a kind of sugar.\n\n## Where Photosynthesis Happens\n- Photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves.\n- Inside the cells of a leaf are small green structures called chloroplasts.\n- They contain a green pigment called chlorophyll.\n- Chlorophyll absorbs light energy from the sun, mostly red and blue light, and reflects green light.\n- This is why leaves look green.\n- Without chlorophyll the plant could not capture the light energy it needs.\n- Without chlorophyll the plant could not capture the light energy it needs\n\n## What Is Released\n- Oxygen is released as a by‑product of photosynthesis.\n- The oxygen leaves the plant through the same tiny openings in the leaves.\n- Animals and people breathe in this oxygen.\n- This is one reason plants matter so much to life on Earth.\n- Animals and people breathe in this oxygen\n\n## The Word Equation\n- The whole process can be written as a word equation.\n- Carbon dioxide plus water, using light energy, makes glucose plus oxygen.\n\n## What Happens When Resources Are Low\n- A plant that gets too little light, too little water or too little carbon dioxide makes less glucose and grows more slowly.\n- This is why a plant kept in a dark cupboard turns pale and weak."
   }
  },
  {
   "profile": "J2",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Two-step equations\n- A two-step equation needs two steps to solve, such as 2x + 3 = 11.\n- The goal is to get the variable x alone on one side of the equals sign.\n- To do that we undo the operations that have been done to x, using the opposite operation.\n- Step one undoes addition or subtraction.\n- In 2x + 3 = 11 the number 3 is added, so subtract 3 from both sides to get 2x = 8.\n- Step two undoes multiplication or division.\n- Here x is multiplied by 2, so divide both sides by 2 to get x = 4.\n- The most important rule is that whatever you do to one side you must do to the other side.\n- An equation is like a balanced scale; doing the same thing to both sides keeps it balanced.\n- Always check your answer by putting it back into the original equation.\n- For x = 4, 2 times 4 plus 3 is 8 plus 3, which is 11; both sides match.\n- Second example: 3x - 5 = 10, add 5 to get 3x = 15, then divide by 3 to get x = 5.",
    "mirror": "## What a two-step equation is\n- A two-step equation needs two steps to solve, such as 2x + 3 = 11.\n\n## Goal of solving\n- The goal is to get the variable x alone on one side of the equals sign.\n\n## Undo operations using opposites\n- We undo the operations that have been done to x by using the opposite operation.\n\n## Step one: undo addition or subtraction\n- In 2x + 3 = 11 the number 3 is added to the term with x, so we subtract 3 from both sides.\n\n## Step two: undo multiplication or division\n- Here x is multiplied by 2, so we divide both sides by 2.\n\n## Core rule: do the same to both sides\n- Whatever you do to one side of the equation you must do to the other side.\n- An equation is like a balanced scale, and doing the same thing to both sides keeps it balanced.\n- If you subtract 3 from only one side, the equation is no longer true.\n\n## Check the answer by substitution\n- Always check your answer by putting it back into the original equation.\n- For x = 4, 2 times 4 plus 3 is 8 plus 3, which is 11.\n- The left side equals the right side, so the answer is correct."
   }
  },
  {
   "profile": "J3",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Long-Term Causes of the War\n- Historians often describe four long-term causes of the First World War, plus one event that set it off.\n- The first is militarism, involving the buildup of large armies and navies by the great powers.\n- Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Generals began to plan for war, and many leaders came to see it as a normal way to settle disputes.\n- The second cause is alliances, which divided Europe into two blocs.\n- The Triple Entente joined France, Russia and Britain, and the Triple Alliance joined Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy.\n- Because of these alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries, so a quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n- The third cause is imperialism, which means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia, which increased distrust between them.\n- The fourth cause is nationalism, strong pride in one's nation or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans, where Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples and Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n\n## The Spark That Set Off the War\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n- Because of the alliances, Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia soon drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain.",
    "mirror": "## The causes of World War I\n- Historians often describe four long-term causes of the First World War, plus one event that set it off.\n- The first is militarism. In the years before 1914 the great powers built up large armies and navies, and Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Generals began to plan for war, and many leaders came to see it as a normal way to settle disputes.\n- The second cause is alliances. Europe was divided into two blocs: Triple Entente (France, Russia, Britain) and Triple Alliance (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy).\n- Because of these alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries, so a quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n- The third cause is imperialism, which means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- The European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia, which increased distrust between them.\n- The fourth cause is nationalism, strong pride in one's nation or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans, where Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples and Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n- Because of the alliances, Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia soon drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain."
   }
  },
  {
   "profile": "J4",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Everyday greetings\n- Picture this: you walk into a Spanish class and say hola to everyone at any time of day.\n- Step 1: Use hola for hello with friends or new people. Next, learn time‑specific greetings.\n- Step 2: Say buenos días for good morning. Next, learn the afternoon greeting.\n- Think of it like a basketball warm‑up: you start with hola, then move to buenos días.\n- Step 3: Say buenas tardes for good afternoon. Next, learn the evening greeting.\n- Step 4: Say buenas noches for good evening or good night. Next, notice gender agreement.\n- Notice that días is masculine plural while tardes and noches are feminine plural, so the endings buenos and buenas change.\n- Think of it like choosing the right video‑game skin: masculine days get buenos, feminine afternoons and nights get buenas.\n\n## Goodbyes\n- Next, learn two ways to say goodbye.\n- Step 1: Say adiós for goodbye. Next, learn the friendly option.\n- Step 2: Say hasta luego for see you later when you expect to see the person again soon. Next, learn how to ask how someone is.\n\n## Asking how someone is\n- Next, learn the informal and formal ways to ask how someone is.\n- Step 1: With friends, family and children use cómo estás for how are you. Next, learn the formal question.\n- Step 2: With older people, strangers or in polite situations use cómo está usted for how are you. Next, learn the usual answer.\n- The usual answer is bien, gracias for fine, thank you.\n\n## Meeting for the first time\n- Next, learn what to say when you meet someone for the first time.\n- Say mucho gusto for nice to meet you.",
    "mirror": "## Basic greetings and time of day\n- Hola means hello and works at any time with friends or new acquaintances.\n- Buenos días is used for good morning.\n- Buenas tardes is used for good afternoon.\n- Buenas noches is used for good evening or good night.\n- Días is masculine plural while tardes and noches are feminine plural, so buenos and buenas change.\n- Think of it like picking the right basketball shoe for the court: the shoe must match the surface just as buenos or buenas must match the gender and number of the noun.\n\n## Goodbyes and asking how someone is\n- Adiós means goodbye and can be used in any situation.\n- Hasta luego means see you later and is friendly when you expect to meet again soon.\n- Think of it like saving your game in a video game: you say hasta luego because you plan to pick up the session again soon.\n- The informal question cómo estás is used with friends, family and children.\n- The formal question cómo está usted is used with older people, strangers or in polite situations.\n- The usual answer is bien, gracias, meaning fine, thank you.\n- Which greeting question do you choose for a teacher you just met?\n- Use the formal cómo está usted because a teacher you just met is a polite situation.\n\n## Meeting someone for the first time\n- Mucho gusto means nice to meet you and is said when meeting someone for the first time.\n- Think of it like signing your name on a new comic page: mucho gusto marks the first encounter on the page."
   }
